{"file":{"content":"\\item \\textbf{On that quotient, \\texttt{sync} has a positive, $Q$-uniform gap.}\nExcluding $\\chi_2$, the worst character sits at fixed frequency $\\xi=1/6$ with\ngap ratio $0.9069$ for every $Q\\in\\{9,27,81,243\\}$ and both tilts, i.e.\n\\[\n\\kappa_Q^{\\text{(sync, non-coboundary)}}\\;=\\;0.0931,\\quad\\text{tilt-independent and $Q$-stable.}\n\\]\nThis is smaller than the pure residue gap $0.236$ (the height-survival mode lifts\nevery sector toward $1$), but it is bounded away from $0$---comfortably\n$\\gtrsim 1/\\mathrm{polylog}(Q)$, which is all H23a needs.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\begin{keystatement}\n\\textbf{Decision.} Run H23a on the faithful \\texttt{sync} skew-product operator,\nrelative to the valuation-pushforward residue reference, restricting the\ncharacter family to the coboundary-quotient. There the finite gap is\n$\\kappa_Q\\approx0.093$, uniform in $Q$ and $s$. The \\texttt{factored} and\nresidue-only operators are kept as \\emph{diagnostics}: they isolate the pure\nresidue decorrelation ($\\kappa=1-\\tfrac{2^{1+s}-1}{2^{1+s}+1}$, stabilising at\n$0.236/0.074$) and certify that $\\chi_2$ is the unique difference between the two.\n\\end{keystatement}\n\n\\paragraph{Immediate follow-ups.}\n(1)~With the operator question settled, the open analytic task is to establish\nthe H23a gap $\\kappa_Q\\gtrsim1/\\mathrm{polylog}(Q)$ for the \\texttt{sync}\noperator on the coboundary-quotient at \\emph{all} $Q$ (numerically $\\approx0.093$\nand flat for $Q\\le3^5$; confirm to larger $Q$ and prove). (2)~Switch the\ndefect-window\nexperiment to multiplicative characters for $3^r$ and measure per-sector","filePath":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/defect_cocycle_with_findings.tex","numLines":28,"startLine":585,"totalLines":835},"type":"text"}